A||||24||BAM-BAM||And you thought that Fiero spark plugs were a pain!!!||||8||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||04-22-2001||09:15 PM|||| I have just spen the entire afternoon changing what ihn my opinion are the hardest spark plugs yet (for me anyway). I have worked on a 65 T-Bird and that was a royal pain, I have worked on a 66 T-Bird and that to was  no different. I have worked on my 85GT Fiero, my 95 Grand Prix, and countless others that don't even rank. But today I got to work on the 89 Caprice Classic Pain in the A$$!!!. Jesus! It took all afternoon, and yes me and GOD shared some conversation. I wouldn't envy this on anyone. Needless to say, the back four were original. Go Figure!!<P>Peace, all better now, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Neverfinished||04-22-2001||09:28 PM||||My favorite was a 94 Chevy Astro van...<BR>with a hot engine.<BR>good times good times||||reg||1
Z||000002||baldlobo||04-22-2001||10:04 PM||||the hardest that I've heard of are:<BR>Mustang Mach1 with the biggest engine in the bay the engine has to be pulled to change the spark plugs.<BR>I forget which car it is but one classic car you have to pull the power brake booster to get at the last two plugs on the drivers side.<BR>And yes Astro's are a *****  to change the spark plug on.<BR>That's all for now I'll see if my dad can tell me some more good stories about cars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||04-22-2001||10:31 PM||||Ford Aerostars.  For the back row, you have to take apart the dash and go in through there.  Not fun.  I have a couple mechanic friends who cringe when i bring that up||||reg||1
Z||000004||MoneypitGT||04-22-2001||10:55 PM||||Along the lines of the T-Bird, a early 60's Continental, ouch.<BR>  Chris<P>------------------<BR><B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects||||reg||4
Z||000005||Nashco||04-23-2001||12:24 AM||||I second the mini-vans...especially astro van front left cylinder...it's a real bugger.  I now know a trick, but I'm not telling, it's my secret!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||BAM-BAM||04-23-2001||12:29 AM|||| Damn NASHCO, Is this your AREA51 secret??? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'll tell you guys, I have never had to work on a MiniVan and I hope I never do. I find it funny though, after going through the maintenance records, The plugs supposedly had been replaced once; I DOUBT IT!!! Found the smae thing on my Fiero. The spark plugs on the front bank were almost burned to a nub. Factory originals they were. Bosch in the rear, AC Delco in the front, or what was left of them. <P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000007||JSocha||04-23-2001||12:42 AM||||Automotive Engineers, got a love em for their ignorant "crammed engine compartment" designs!  <P>Gotta wonder if they also send the respective automotive mechanics instructions on how to service them with a manual written in "Chinese"?<P>My wife's 1997 Dodge 1500 pickup that I bought her, they put the fuel filter inside the gas tank and the mechanics will not change it even though they know its plugged and needs to be replaced!  Engineering marvel at its best!?!?!?!?!?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||04-23-2001||01:10 AM||||Yeah, it's almost a sure bet that the hard to get to spark plug is what's making you need a tune up.  When you take it to a shop (not any shop I've ever worked at, that's for damn sure, I wouldn't let this happen) some guys will just skip it.  The hard to get to one usually is toast, while the others are fine.  I did a tune up on somebody's astro that couldn't believe how much better their car ran after this tune up...they had just got one 15k miles ago..hehe <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Sorry I can't tell you my secret...guys gotta make a livin' don't he?  Trade secret...thanks dad <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Neverfinished||04-23-2001||01:15 AM||||I don't know if I'm ruining anyones secret but for the last two spark plugs on the astro van I just went through the inner fender with a couple extensions and universal joints... Still a b*tch though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||04-23-2001||09:01 AM||||I have a '94 Safari with the enhanced engine.  I don't remember the plugs being that much more difficult than many other cars.  I got to most of them from underneath while laying on a creeper.  Seems like it took less than an hour to do all 6.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||04-23-2001||10:37 AM||||I used to have an 88 Firebird with a 305. Had to get the plugs out of <I>that</I> from underneath, too.<BR>Also used to have one of the infamous V-8 Chevy Monzas that required the motor to be jacked up to change plugs. (Headers actually made the job easier.)<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000012||MontanaFiero||04-23-2001||01:08 PM||||I remember trying to do a late 80's Ford fullsize van. One of the plugs was right in line with the firewall and you couldn't even get the socket on it. I don't remember what we finally did to get that s.o.b. out of there, but it involved small fingers.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Spektyr||04-23-2001||02:20 PM||||Changed the plugs on a guy at school's 80-something Firebird or Trans Am (way back when I was in highschool).  Don't remember exactly what it was, because at the time the only car that existed to me was the IROC Camaro.  (go figure)<P>To get the number 8 plug I fed the wrench up handle first from the bottom, worked it onto the plug, and then he racheted it ONE CLICK at a time while I held it against the plug.<P>Only took 15 minutes to get that plug.<P>After 2 hours of cursing before we thought of this method...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierofreek2001||04-23-2001||11:11 PM||||my friend has a 94 stealth twin turbo, and it takes him about 4 hours to get to 3 of the spark plugs... what a pain, i would never change them ! lol<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||danny boy||04-23-2001||11:28 PM||||Damn, beat me to it. I changed plugs on the Stealth(GTO!GTO!) and the upper intake has to come off along with all kinds of other things bolted to it.The engine is horizontally mounted and the back bank of the engine isn't even visible. Oddly enough when I upgraded turbos the front turbo took 2 days while the rear took about 4 hours. And then I broke the tranny.Bummer.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Nashco||04-24-2001||12:27 AM||||Well, I don't know if you'd have grounds to *****  about having to work on your stealth...I don't mind a hard-to-work-with design if the performance of the car is worth it.  Now, when it's a mini-van or any of the common mass-quantities vehicles out there, those things can be a nuisance.  Hell, I'd do a six hour spark plug job if I could test drive a twin turbo stealth for a while <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I'm a speed-whore, what can I say?||||reg||1
Z||000017||thomas_l||04-24-2001||09:09 AM||||I'll second the 83-up Camaro - just a notch worse than a Caprice. My mom had an 88 5.7L IROC for about 8 years and I worked in a shop at the time - so I did 2 or 3 tune-ups during the time she owned it. #8 comes out from underneath on the IROC. You can get it from up top in the Caprice. The best combo for me is a long handled swivel head Snap-on ratchet with a swivel plug socket on it, and maybe a 1" extension. Sometimes you have to use a regular 5/8" deep socket to break loose (or just break!) the tough ones so you have a little "wobble" room. The only thing that really comes close was the first Ford Aerostars. At least on the Astro you can use a long extension thru the wheel well.||||reg||12
Z||000018||rogergarrison||04-24-2001||09:46 AM||||those mustangs were with the 428/429. Didnt have to pull the engine, but did have to take out motor mounts and jack it up to change them. Im glad mopars finally starting to put engines in rite instead of sideways, make it lots easier. Ive always though access panels in wheel wheels would be realy great. On fieros why didnt they just make a removeable panel behind the passenger seat? so much work would have been so much easier. Has anyone ever tried making one. My next kit i think im gonna try it, cant be that big a deal.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||pontiacs4me||04-24-2001||09:11 PM||||I noticed cars with small blocks were mentioned alot.  If chevy didnt put the damn spark plugs on the bottom of the engine that wouldnt be such a problem. thats why I like my pontiac v8's, sparkplugs above the exhaust ports instead of under them.  I met a guy at our local racetrack that had one of those big block fords in a mustang, he drilled 1" holes in his inner fenderwells to access the sparkplugs.  Seemed to work ok.||||reg||1
Z||000020||jaguiler||04-24-2001||10:43 PM||||92 BMW 325i - you have to remove the plastic cover over the engine... then unscrew everything else that is on top of spark plug.... there is always one out of the 36 screws that get lost.....<BR>it is very easy to do.. but it hurts your back a little from the bending over....  but nothing compared to the fiero...on the BMW I get my hands a little dirty maybe just up to my fingers...on the Fiero my whole body gets full of oil and greese....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000021||ray b||04-25-2001||06:01 PM||||unversial joints sometimes 3 or 4,many differnt extension and a short handel rachit all help to get in tight spaces.||||reg||1
Z||000022||KRMFiero||04-25-2001||06:56 PM||||My dad's Ford Aerostar sucks with plugs, or my friend Matts 90-92? Ford SHO 6cyl   you have to pull the intake and eveything off....||||reg||1
Z||000023||Wade87GT||04-25-2001||07:19 PM||||My friend has a mint 87 Supra. The engine is a straight 6 and the cast intake manifold lays right across the center two sparkplugs. Of course, anytime you do a full tune-up, you have to buy an manifold gaskit kit.||||reg||1
Z||000024||StuGood||04-25-2001||07:37 PM||||You guys think you've got it tough.  A while back, I had to change the spark plug in my Briggs and Stratton lawn mower.  Had to remember "lefty loosey," dropped the plug on mower deck once, even had to gap the new plug by hand.  Definitely one of the most frustrating, miserable five minutes of maintenance ever performed in my entire life.  <BR>Then, to add insult to injury, right away after that I had to mow the lawn.  <P>Just had to throw that in.  Feel free to throw it out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
